{"name":"Oxygen Blue","hex":"#92b6d5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"oxygen","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The chemical element (symbol O) with an atomic number of 8 and relative atomic mass of 15.9994. It is a colorless and odorless gas. Sometimes called elemental oxygen to distinguish it from molecular oxygen.","Molecular oxygen (O2), a colorless, odorless gas at room temperature."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/oxygen"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Oxygen","blocks":[{"text":"Oxygen is a chemical element; it has the symbol O and its atomic number is 8. It is a member of the chalcogen group in the periodic table. It is highly reactive, a nonmetal, and a potent oxidizing agent that readily forms oxides with most elements as well as with other compounds. Oxygen is the most abundant element in Earth's crust, making up almost half of the Earth's crust in the form of various oxides such as water, carbon dioxide, iron oxides, and silicates. It is also the third-most abundant element in the universe after hydrogen and helium."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/Liquid_oxygen_in_a_beaker_%28cropped_and_retouched%29.jpg/330px-Liquid_oxygen_in_a_beaker_%28cropped_and_retouched%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oxygen","wikidata":"Q629"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/PriestleyFuseli.jpg/330px-PriestleyFuseli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/PriestleyFuseli.jpg/330px-PriestleyFuseli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Among several contemporaries who had made discoveries independently from one another, Joseph Priestley was the first to publish his findings on oxygen.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APriestleyFuseli.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Lavoisier%2C_Antoine_Laurent_de_%281743-1794%29_CIPB1171.jpg/500px-Lavoisier%2C_Antoine_Laurent_de_%281743-1794%29_CIPB1171.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/Lavoisier%2C_Antoine_Laurent_de_%281743-1794%29_CIPB1171.jpg/500px-Lavoisier%2C_Antoine_Laurent_de_%281743-1794%29_CIPB1171.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Antoine Lavoisier discredited the phlogiston theory.","link":"https: